[38;5;231m⏺[39m That's the feature defined. Two more questions, then I'll present the design.

  How notifications actually reach the user. These aren't mutually exclusive — real apps usually combine them — so pick
  any that fit.

  - [1mBell icon + unread count + dropdown[0m — the durable surface. Notifications persist, have read/unread state, survive
    you being away from the screen. This is the one that makes the feature feel like a feature, and it's where the
    watcher list pays off.
  - [1mToasts[0m — transient corner popups when something happens while you're looking. Great for demos because the reaction
    is immediate and visible. Useless for anything that happened while you were elsewhere, so it's a complement, not a
    substitute.
  - [1mA dedicated activity page[0m — full history, filterable. Most useful at volume; in a prototype with a dozen events it's
    mostly an empty page with good intentions.
  - [1mBrowser/OS notifications[0m — the real [38;5;153mNotification[39m API. Genuinely impressive in a demo, but needs a permission prompt,
    behaves differently across browsers, and by spec is suppressed or redundant when the tab is focused — which in a
    prototype is [3malways[0m. High effort-to-insight ratio.

  [1mMy recommendation: bell + dropdown as the backbone, plus toasts.[0m Together they cover both "it happened just now" and
  "it happened while I was away," which is the whole emotional range of the feature, and they share one data source so
  the second one is nearly free.

